      "answer211": {
        "bothPrint": "b_k >= q_k - 1 >= z_k for 0 <= k < n",
        "cn223Definition": "q'_k counts distinct nonzero (n-k)-order subtables, and (123) gives q'_k >= z_k",
        "singleCounterexample": {
          "matrix": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ],
          "function": "x_1",
          "k": 0,
          "b_k": 1,
          "q_k": 1,
          "q'_k": 1,
          "z_k": 1,
          "printedChain": "1 >= 0 >= 1"
        },
        "reason": "At a level k < n, if a cofactor is nonzero there is a valid set of the remaining rows. Flipping x_(k+1) in that set removes or adds a row with a 1, so it cannot stay an exact cover. Thus each distinct nonzero subtable contributes a level-k BDD node, and the existing (123) supplies the second inequality.",
        "minimalReading": "b_k >= q'_k >= z_k, with the original range and no-all-zero-row hypothesis"
      },
      "answer217": {
        "bothPrint": "conjunction of S_1(X_j) for j from 1 through 468",
        "cn224Definition": "The n columns index covered cells, and the m decision variables x_i index rows (placements). Formula (129) has one S_1(X_j) for each column. The chessboard has 8*8 = 64 cells; adding one- and three-cell pieces only extends the placement rows.",
